# Outputs Open When The Member Asks

An output arriving is **not** a request to look at it. A turn can surface ten
outputs and the surface must not move: no module opens, nothing docks, and the
chat stays exactly the width it already had. The output chip in the transcript
is the only thing that opens an output module.

This is app-wide and permanent. It holds for every chat module on every page.

## What this means on the surface

- Ask something that produces charts, tables, boards or a report → the answer
  reads, the output chips land under it, the closing intent chips trail them.
  The right-hand module stays shut and the chat keeps the whole row.
- Tap an output chip → **that** is when the output module opens, and when the
  chat docks to make room for it.
- Tap a version thumbnail on a chip → the same module opens on that version.
- Come back later and tap a chip from three turns ago → it opens then. An
  output the member never asked to see is never forced on screen.
- Nothing about the answer's own wording opens a module. "Opened the full
  ranking in Results & Details →" is narration, not an instruction.

## What still opens a module

Only a member action, because the action *is* the ask:

- An output chip, or a version thumbnail on one.
- A footnote / citation marker — tapping it is the ask to see References.
- Expand / full-screen on an output.
- Generating a report from the member's own selection.

## A control chip is not an output

There are two kinds of chip and they behave differently. A chip that asks a
**question** ("Compare these cookies", "Build me a report") gets an answer and
a row of output chips — nothing opens. A chip that names a **control**
("Claim your products", "Update your brand logo", "Filter my portfolio by
Guiding Stars") fires that control, because firing it is the whole ask.

A control chip may still let the narration read first and act a beat later —
that is pacing, and the member already asked. What is banned is a surface the
member never asked for arriving on screen on its own.

If you cannot tell which kind a chip is, read its label. If the label names a
thing to look at rather than a thing to do, it is a question chip and its
results belong behind an output chip.

## How it is wired

`openOutputPane(dest)` in `pages/wiseai.html` is the one door. Every opener
above goes through it; nothing on the surfacing path calls it.

- `postSurfaceBlock` and `insertAllVersionSlides` write blocks into a **closed**
  pane. Writing is not opening.
- `openPane` takes `open: false` by default. Pass `open: true` only from a path
  the member asked for.
- There is no `openModuleByDefault` on the mount. The chat's `onOpenModule` hook
  stays wired, because a transcript restored from history can still carry a
  visible open-module chip — but only a tap fires it.
- Blocks written into a closed pane have no box to measure, so their charts,
  tables and compare boards are booted by `openOutputPane` on the way in, not
  when they were inserted.

## Resizing

Docking the chat is part of *opening*, not part of surfacing. The chat may
collapse from the full row to its column at the moment the member opens an
output — that is the room the output needs. It must never move because an
output merely arrived, and a turn must never widen or narrow the chat on its
own. Only the member's width toggle changes the chat's rest width.

## Rules for the agent

- Never open a pane, panel, board or overlay from `onReply`, `onReplyDone`, a
  surface/queue function, or anything else on the answer's own path.
- Never re-add a "reveal the first output once the transcript finishes" step.
  Finishing the transcript is not the member asking.
- A new surface that produces outputs must post an output chip for each one.
  An output with no chip is unreachable — that is a bug, not a quiet output.
- Do not fork this per page. If a host needs it, it wires the same door — see
  the nothing-page-local rule.
- Outputs still land in stage order behind the answer (see the
  turn-stage-order rule). Ordering and opening are separate questions; this
  rule only says the pane stays shut.

## Verify

`scripts/_output_pacing_check.js` guards the wiring.
`scripts/_output_open_probe.py` drives a real turn and checks that every pane
stayed shut and the chat never resized, then taps the output chip and checks
that the module opens. Run it in both themes after touching any of this.
